WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.330
- Hi everyone.

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:06.330
So last year I made a video
about India's JEE Advanced exam,

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.120
but I don't think that
I really did it justice.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.370
So for this video,

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:13.506
I wanted to invite some experts
in science and engineering

00:00:13.506 --> 00:00:16.150
to share their thoughts on the exam.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:19.110
They spoke about the
difficulty of the questions,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.860
whether they thought the exam
would prepare students well

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.600
for the future, and also
shared their thoughts

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.490
on exams, as a whole.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.860
I also spoke to a couple of
Indian students who took the JEE

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.620
and are now doing research
work here in Australia.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.070
For those not familiar,

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.320
the JEE exam is an admissions exam

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.880
into engineering colleges,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.260
and it's set by over a million students

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.450
competing for just a few thousand places.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:50.560
You sit two papers, each three
hours long on the same day,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.370
and it's all multi-choice.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.249
So with that, let's hear what
people had to say about it.

00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:58.419
(smooth jazz music)

00:00:58.419 --> 00:01:01.410
- I think this is a,
it's a very tough exam.

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.760
So if I was a student at high school,

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.900
and if this was intimidating,
when you first read it,

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:09.451
I would not be surprised.

00:01:09.451 --> 00:01:12.030
And if it's not at all
intimidating for you,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.890
then I would say congratulations,

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.420
'cause you're a very
impressive kid. (chuckles)

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.950
- Well, it's really, really interesting.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.900
- It looks pretty difficult.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.980
For example, the question in physics,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:28.170
I know I, couldn't probably
can't answer, you know,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:30.870
maybe most of it now.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.200
Though, maybe 30 years back, I could do.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.610
- You know, these exams seem you know,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:40.220
they're very long, they
have a very wide variety

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.310
of questions they cover and they're hard.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.380
I think any student who
comes up against these exams

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.570
is gonna feel pretty
lousy by the end of it.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:51.270
- I'd probably you know
leave the exam room crying

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.220
if I was in year 12 and had to do this.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.560
Yeah.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.085
Good luck, good luck. (laughing)

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:02.085
(smooth jazz music)

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.620
- So when we compare this JEE exam

00:02:06.578 --> 00:02:10.660
with some of the subjects
that are taught here

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.280
in some of our universities in Australia.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.590
So here is a question
that I was looking at,

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:17.600
the depth of, questions that are asked

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.110
can be one difficult for students

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:22.410
in the first or second
year who are studying

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.870
in a bachelor level in Australia.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.400
- It's really quite ambitious
that they're trying to test

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.010
chemistry, and physics, and math,

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.830
and the paper is laid out fairly nicely

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:36.680
in that you have to earn
one mark per minute.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.070
Looking at the questions,

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.730
a lot of them are in some
sense, sort of elementary

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.200
in the sense that they use

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.160
the kind of math taught in high schools.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.160
Although in some areas, I would
say the syllabus in calculus

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:53.830
and a couple of other
areas, go somewhat beyond

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:55.640
what is currently taught
in Australian schools,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.010
or currently examined
in Australian schools.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.310
But a lot of the questions
are basically tricky,

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.170
or slightly sneaky, but
also don't necessarily

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:08.660
require you to work through
the question in full detail.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.082
It was multiple choice questions.

00:03:11.082 --> 00:03:13.070
You have to simply decide,

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.980
can I reject this possibility or not?

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.440
There is some attempt to
stop people gaming that way

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:23.350
by applying penalties
to some wrong answers,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.530
but still it's very much
a race against the clock.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:31.298
- I think the mathematic
questions are clever.

00:03:31.298 --> 00:03:36.298
It looks for the person's
understanding of the matter,

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.430
just rather than doing
some direct questions.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:46.430
So in that sense, I,
you know, they're okay,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.240
they can be done.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.433
And you know, some of these concepts

00:03:51.433 --> 00:03:56.433
I do even use in my course
here in Master schools here,

00:03:57.522 --> 00:04:02.522
especially those involved with
a system of linear equations

00:04:05.416 --> 00:04:07.420
and determinants and those concepts.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:10.411
- So concepts like high spin and low spin,

00:04:10.411 --> 00:04:12.360
you know again are complex.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.470
That's something that at
the university of Melbourne

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.790
in first year chemistry we
would treat in second semester.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.050
There was a question on
essentially Arrhenius equation.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:24.950
So rates of, a rate of
reaction as a function of,

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.100
free energy, of the keeps free energy.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:27.933
So here we go.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:29.490
It asks you something like
the activation energy,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.020
the backward reaction exceeds

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.610
that of the forward reaction by 2RT.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.180
So again you've got, basically
I think you you'd have to,

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:38.013
sketch this out.

00:04:38.013 --> 00:04:41.050
You'd have to think about,
okay, if the back reaction

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.240
is exceeding the forward reaction,

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.010
what's the reaction free energy?

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.590
So it's not just the
concepts that are difficult.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:50.300
I think it's asking you in a
way that is, that is tricky.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.400
You have to think about,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:52.890
it's not just enough to know the equation

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.419
and you know, throw in the numbers.

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:56.814
You've got to think about
it before you get the number

00:04:56.814 --> 00:04:57.800
that you're throwing into the equation.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.990
And obviously, and they're not letting you

00:04:58.990 --> 00:04:59.903
have a calculator,

00:05:01.438 --> 00:05:04.086
so I guess (laughing)
another challenge there.

00:05:04.086 --> 00:05:07.086
(smooth piano jazz)

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.660
- Definitely this will set up a basic,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.543
like a background and a baseline.

00:05:16.565 --> 00:05:18.920
So you could imagine
that all the IIT students

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:23.920
who have come with, they would
have had from fundamentals.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.100
But from then onward there
are so many things to learn,

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:30.350
because after you come to IITs

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.170
there is still, you know,

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.080
only it will be like a bell curve, again.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.470
You know, for example,
you give an exam to a,

00:05:40.796 --> 00:05:41.940
you know, a very tough exam,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.190
and choose the best in the world

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.430
and then give them them another exam.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:47.980
And still, again,

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.890
the performance is
going to be like a bell.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.050
But this happened to be,
this is kind of something

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.530
that is, it's kind of basically needed,

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.973
basic foundations for engineering.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.830
But I would guess, nearly
the majority of them

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.140
would be ready to do
for, you know, go for,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.720
especially general education.

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.257
And that, explains why, for example,

00:06:10.257 --> 00:06:12.186
IIT students are you know,

00:06:12.186 --> 00:06:15.343
IIT graduates are doing
well out on the world.

00:06:15.343 --> 00:06:16.176
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:06:16.176 --> 00:06:18.630
- I had to look at a
couple of the one-hour

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.710
math sections of papers.

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.900
And I would say I would
be fairly challenged to,

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.773
to get a decent result
on those, in an hour.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:29.770
Partly because as a
professional mathematician,

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.290
I'm interested in
actually telling a story,

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:33.480
when I answer a problem.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:34.590
I want to lay out

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.280
why the answer I'm
producing is the answer.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.770
Simply being able to say
well the answer is 27,

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:42.930
to me is not so helpful or so interesting.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:45.750
And in terms of future success

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:49.920
in mathematics or indeed in
many scientific disciplines,

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.920
the ability to reason properly,

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:56.610
and to explain your reasoning clearly

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.860
is terribly, terribly important.

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:02.670
So while I have no reason to believe,

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.580
that using the exam the way it's used,

00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:08.170
isn't selecting very bright students

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.083
for the engineering colleges
that they're going to.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.483
It's not clear to me that it's
the optimal selector of that.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:21.449
And there is also the ugly question of,

00:07:21.449 --> 00:07:26.449
of coaching and unequal
access to resources.

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:31.610
We all know that in
any educational system,

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.230
if you go to a good school,

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.680
well-resourced with the best
teachers and so on like that,

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.410
you expect a better outcome.

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.580
That's ultimately the main
justification for spending money

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:44.400
on education as a society,
that if you work harder at it,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.230
you produce better results.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.970
But with these race against
the clock style examinations,

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.750
there's a trade-off

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.430
between the students'
ability in the subject,

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:58.100
natural intelligence and
other things like that.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.800
And they're having been trained

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:02.100
to do a bit examinations of this type.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.330
- Exams can have different
purpose and entry exams

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:09.470
that are sort of in a form
of a drill type of questions,

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.170
usually require students to prepare

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.480
a lot before to practice.

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.720
Brain is like any other muscle,

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:18.260
the more you practice
it, the better it gets.

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.590
So when you have a large pool
of candidates and only small

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.320
number of spaces,
usually have drill exams,

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:25.430
because what they're showing

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.980
is whether the candidate
is serious enough,

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:29.800
whether they're able to practice,

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.783
and how they perform on the time limited,

00:08:33.783 --> 00:08:37.300
pressured environment conditions.

00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:40.418
They're not necessarily a
prediction whether that candidate

00:08:40.418 --> 00:08:44.243
will then do very well in
the next step of education.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:47.030
- So right now I have,

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:50.130
I know two students who
from India's technology,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.320
one from Delhi, other, from a Madras.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.900
So right now they are here in (mumbles)

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.110
applying AI, artificial intelligence,

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.510
and deep learning techniques

00:09:01.391 --> 00:09:03.260
for medical application.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.244
So this work, they're able
to do within, you know,

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:07.110
certain of time.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:10.733
They demonstrate their skills and ability.

00:09:10.733 --> 00:09:13.733
(smooth jazz music)

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.610
- Maybe a train is moving at
this velocity, at this speed,

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.930
we need to consider,
is it really possible?

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.230
Is it feasible?

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:28.690
And then there are
questions about experience.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.840
Have I actually seen a wide
variance, more or less of this?

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.313
Is it possible?
- Yeah, experience.

00:09:34.313 --> 00:09:35.470
Is this really possible?

00:09:35.470 --> 00:09:38.400
So those type of questions
actually come from experience.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:41.630
So we actually have to try out
various experiments in lab.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:44.603
You have to know that what
exactly are the ranges,

00:09:45.595 --> 00:09:47.670
a practicality that exists in science.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.600
- Two marks or maybe half
mark can change your rank

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:53.050
by great amount, it's possible.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.790
Like, I think minus two
marks can decrease the rank

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.960
by 200 or 400.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.690
So you need to be very
careful about how to attempt,

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:02.673
which question to attempt

00:10:02.673 --> 00:10:05.730
and you need to be very sure
about it, attempt question.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:07.460
- Maybe you have for other students

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:12.210
that are preparing for JEE,
feel that being open-minded

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:15.350
is what would really help right now.

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.090
All those that are thinking

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:20.250
of maybe starting in class 10, or nine.

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.660
I feel that they need to be open-minded.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.321
They need to be considering that yes,

00:10:25.321 --> 00:10:28.540
experience in lives are very
important, apart from studies.

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:31.201
I feel that those
experience can really help,

00:10:31.201 --> 00:10:33.260
not only in JEE, but also in life.

00:10:34.853 --> 00:10:37.190
And yeah, enjoying life is
very important, with studies.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:40.200
- One of my thing would be,

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.740
don't be scared of the exam
hall, examination hall.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:48.190
So I've experienced that
in JEE, that's not good.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:51.570
So be open, be free,
whatever you're studying,

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:54.093
just pretend to think and yeah,

00:10:54.093 --> 00:10:55.393
all the rest, all happens.

00:10:56.273 --> 00:10:59.273
(smooth jazz music)

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:01.460
- Yeah. So when I looked through it,

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.293
there was a lot of the questions that,

00:11:03.293 --> 00:11:05.750
they are based very much
on this sort of principle

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:07.370
of memorization and recitation,

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:11.120
which I think is just
an extraordinarily bad

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.300
educational tool because it assumes

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:17.170
that everyone has the exact
same capabilities in that area.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:21.040
And as someone who never had a
great memory for these things

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.603
and had to work extra hard.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:27.360
and then became a professional
physicist for over a decade,

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:30.210
you realize very quickly
that those particular skills

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.240
don't help you much at all,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:34.440
once you're actually
working as a scientist

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.820
because you know,
scientists get to the point

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:40.130
where the material they're working with

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.480
is so complicated, you
just don't remember.

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:44.030
No one could remember it.

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:46.660
So that, skill of
memorization and recitation

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.050
is not as helpful as people might think.

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:54.050
In science you know, you tend
to not be successful every day

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:55.380
of your working career.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:56.550
In fact, quite the opposite.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:59.840
You tend to, you know, not get
anywhere for quite a while.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:02.185
I mean, you'll have one lucky day

00:12:02.185 --> 00:12:04.270
where something will
occur and your training

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:05.640
will allow you to see that

00:12:05.640 --> 00:12:07.800
and identify it as being special.

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:09.250
And that's what makes a good scientist,

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:12.119
but for the other 360
odd days of the year,

00:12:12.119 --> 00:12:15.283
you'll have a pretty rough time.

00:12:16.753 --> 00:12:17.880
And those sorts of skills,

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.340
those sorts of resilience skills,

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.200
and understanding how
science and engineering work,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:24.080
you know the process of falsification

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.570
is not something that is tested at all

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:29.460
in these sorts of examinations.

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.780
And, it's unfortunate
because you want people

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:34.800
with those skills more than
you want people with skills

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.620
with memorization capabilities.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:41.760
- When I looked at the
JEE math, the maths exams,

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:45.100
it led me to recall an interesting article

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.450
by the great British
mathematician, G. H. Hardy.

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:51.991
Indian viewers of this blog,

00:12:51.991 --> 00:12:54.675
will perhaps be well
acquainted with Hardy,

00:12:54.675 --> 00:12:59.270
who is generally regarded as responsible

00:12:59.270 --> 00:13:02.561
for bringing the great Indian
mathematician, Ramanujan

00:13:02.561 --> 00:13:04.050
to the attention of the world.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:08.090
And so Indian viewers should
have some affection for Hardy.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.690
You have to understand that
when Hardy was a young man

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:13.610
and through the early
stages of his career,

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:16.840
mathematical education
in the United Kingdom

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.900
was governed by the
Mathematical Tripos examination

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:22.290
for Cambridge undergraduates.

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:25.173
This was a highly competitive examination.

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:28.390
An examination of great difficulty,

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:30.050
in the sense that it
required a large amount

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:32.330
of preparation and training,

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.470
paying for professional
coaching, and so on like that.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.530
And on the basis of your Tripos result,

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.820
this could lead to academic possibilities,

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:44.625
but also to high positions
in the British Civil Service

00:13:44.625 --> 00:13:47.150
and the Colonial Office
and things like that.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.490
In fact, it was viewed generically

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:52.530
as some kind of way of detecting

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:56.480
deep intellectual superiority
and ranking people.

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:00.000
And Hardy didn't have much time for that.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.040
Hardy was actually quite
a fan of examinations

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.900
and quite a fan of lecturing, as I am.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:07.800
And this is unfashionable these days.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.930
What he was not comfortable
with was the Tripos,

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:12.930
particularly in the first version,

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:14.280
in which he encountered it.

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:17.000
In order for the exam
to work the way it did,

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:18.863
a kind of culture had built up,

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:21.380
in terms of the setting of the questions,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:23.910
the kinds of questions,
and so on like that.

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:27.470
And most of these had very
little to do with mathematics

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:29.120
that was either practically used

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.700
or intellectually interesting,

00:14:30.700 --> 00:14:32.760
or anything recent in mathematics.

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:36.450
It was basically playing certain games.

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:39.760
The examiners would try and
set slightly sneaky questions

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:42.410
on certain topics, and students would hope

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.260
to have practiced lots of questions

00:14:44.260 --> 00:14:46.040
of a vaguely similar type

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.020
in the hope that they
could get through this.

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:49.540
And Hardy saw this,

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:52.380
as actually warping mathematical education

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:53.740
in the United Kingdom.

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:58.470
So Hardy's argument was,
examinations of the Tripos style

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:03.780
are not a very good way to
rank people intellectually,

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:05.590
and therefore perhaps,

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.430
one might consider doing
things differently.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:10.610
It would be drawing a long bow to claim

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:13.100
that the JEE Advanced Examination

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:15.133
suffers from the defects of the Tripos.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.763
Nonetheless, there are
elements of analogy there,

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.490
particularly the issue
of expensive coaching

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.920
and the diversion of students

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:31.930
away from studying subjects
and developing their abilities,

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:36.150
and instead teaching them to
do certain kinds of questions

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:39.730
at great speed, which is not actually,

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:43.610
something of great use either
in the practical applications

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:45.630
of mathematics in the real world,

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:48.370
or in academic mathematics,

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:50.563
scientific research or scholarship.

00:15:51.778 --> 00:15:54.778
(smooth jazz music)

